{"classification":"mathematical-duplicated-approximant","correction_applied":true,"id":"O007-CORR-0127","math_ordinal":363,"provenance":{"basis":"exact current source-correction ledger row","kind":"source-correction-ledger","source_resource_ids":["O007-RESOURCE-CH24-SOURCE-CORRECTIONS"]},"rationale":"The analogous construction and the immediately following use of both approximants require separate bounds for g and h; repeating g leaves h uncontrolled.","record_type":"source_correction","rights_id":"O007-RIGHTS-FREMLIN-DSL","schema_version":"1.1.0","source_locator":"authority/fremlin/source/mt2.2016/mt244.tex:420-421","source_normalized_sha256":"0dd4cb67188e625972cbce612786b4e49aeeba8b96fc06aee06d53bf5c913595","source_text":"two bounds both use integral_X |f-g|^p <= epsilon^p","target_locator":"source/id-ID/mt244.tex:433-434","target_normalized_sha256":"99442ac460e51f961847a761b009e2ffd3b200bbfd1ea116021ce6b8a440763c","target_text":"second bound uses integral_X |f-h|^p <= epsilon^p","unit_id":"O007-FREMLIN-V2-S244"}
{"classification":"mathematical-wrong-function-space","correction_applied":true,"id":"O007-CORR-0128","math_ordinal":499,"provenance":{"basis":"exact current source-correction ledger row","kind":"source-correction-ledger","source_resource_ids":["O007-RESOURCE-CH24-SOURCE-CORRECTIONS"]},"rationale":"The argument begins with f in L^p, h acts on L^p, and the vanishing property is required for L^p. L^q is the wrong space.","record_type":"source_correction","rights_id":"O007-RIGHTS-FREMLIN-DSL","schema_version":"1.1.0","source_locator":"authority/fremlin/source/mt2.2016/mt244.tex:576-578","source_normalized_sha256":"e5ccc76a57d193eeac2f969533a205228be803d8bd1165fdbf996186c37d7fd0","source_text":"f belongs to \\eusm L^q","target_locator":"source/id-ID/mt244.tex:594-596","target_normalized_sha256":"26a37273984ba6ca72492c96fdefed9c843137a7a36ec572d8bd667f46dc0ad0","target_text":"f belongs to \\eusm L^p","unit_id":"O007-FREMLIN-V2-S244"}
{"classification":"mathematical-wrong-least-upper-bound-variable","correction_applied":true,"id":"O007-CORR-0129","math_ordinal":661,"provenance":{"basis":"exact current source-correction ledger row","kind":"source-correction-ledger","source_resource_ids":["O007-RESOURCE-CH24-SOURCE-CORRECTIONS"]},"rationale":"w is an arbitrary upper bound; the constructed w_1 has just been proved to be the least upper bound.","record_type":"source_correction","rights_id":"O007-RIGHTS-FREMLIN-DSL","schema_version":"1.1.0","source_locator":"authority/fremlin/source/mt2.2016/mt244.tex:784-785","source_normalized_sha256":"ba32e5a39253ed5f0c3180a2e50f03386be474ce4a77aeba2c534f263f27eeed","source_text":"w=\\sup A'=\\sup A","target_locator":"source/id-ID/mt244.tex:807-808","target_normalized_sha256":"5eb9962a3eadf67243193a3bfa0b4d41a0124f4ac85262e0a152322648dfe8f9","target_text":"w_1=\\sup A'=\\sup A","unit_id":"O007-FREMLIN-V2-S244"}
{"classification":"mathematical-wrong-derivative-term","correction_applied":true,"id":"O007-CORR-0130","math_ordinal":782,"provenance":{"basis":"exact current source-correction ledger row","kind":"source-correction-ledger","source_resource_ids":["O007-RESOURCE-CH24-SOURCE-CORRECTIONS"]},"rationale":"The duplicated term would make the derivative zero and contradict the next equality. The first term must have +1 to factor to the displayed phi_0' identity.","record_type":"source_correction","rights_id":"O007-RIGHTS-FREMLIN-DSL","schema_version":"1.1.0","source_locator":"authority/fremlin/source/mt2.2016/mt244.tex:927-930","source_normalized_sha256":"f74ec2606d5230a1dbbfc4550af7125b84b8828fdc88211827f16c7f9a1581aa","source_text":"phi_1' subtracts two identical (1/t-1)^(p-2) terms","target_locator":"source/id-ID/mt244.tex:951-954","target_normalized_sha256":"347a4a69815026cfddc3adf1097828385225166af3e3d841af6247a63377e7fa","target_text":"first term is (1/t+1)^(p-2)","unit_id":"O007-FREMLIN-V2-S244"}
